---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { dataMode } from '../lib/data';
---
<Base title="Methodology">
  <div class="shell page-intro"><div class="section-label">HOW WE TRACK / METHODOLOGY</div><h1>Evidence before<br /><em>assumption.</em></h1><p>TrackYourInfra is designed as a public record. A useful update needs a source, a date and enough context for someone else to check it.</p></div>
  <div class="shell methodology-layout"><nav class="method-nav" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#demonstration-data">Demonstration data</a><a href="#status">Project status</a><a href="#confidence">Confidence</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a><a href="#contribute">Contribution roadmap</a></nav>
    <div class="method-content">
      <section id="demonstration-data"><div class="section-label">01 / CURRENT DATA</div><h2>Separate demonstration and real records.</h2><p>{dataMode === "demo" ? "The three demo corridors, routes, dates, costs and progress values are invented to test the interface. They are not real project claims." : "The public dataset includes source-backed records. Demo corridors are stored separately. Dashed orange lines, where present, are unverified community-drawn proposals; solid routes require independent review."}</p><p>The first real pilot record is Bengaluru Metro Phase 3. Its accepted route geometry is intentionally absent until a source-backed alignment is reviewed.</p></section>
      <section id="status"><div class="section-label">02 / LIFECYCLE</div><h2>Status describes a stage.</h2><div class="definition-grid"><div><strong>Proposed</strong><p>Publicly discussed; formal approval not established.</p></div><div><strong>Approved</strong><p>Relevant authority has formally approved it.</p></div><div><strong>Funded</strong><p>Funding has been confirmed.</p></div><div><strong>Tendering</strong><p>Procurement is underway.</p></div><div><strong>Under construction</strong><p>Physical work has started.</p></div><div><strong>Partially operational</strong><p>Some sections are open.</p></div><div><strong>Operational</strong><p>The tracked scope is open.</p></div><div><strong>Delayed / cancelled</strong><p>A dated source confirms the change.</p></div></div></section>
      <section id="confidence"><div class="section-label">03 / CONFIDENCE</div><h2>Confidence is about evidence.</h2><p><strong>High</strong> means supported by primary documents or multiple corroborating sources. <strong>Medium</strong> means credible reporting or verified observations. <strong>Low</strong> means an early or illustrative report requiring further review. A confidence label applies to a particular claim, not to every fact on a page.</p></section>
      <section id="sources"><div class="section-label">04 / PROVENANCE</div><h2>Keep the trail visible.</h2><p>For each important claim, the site records its source, publication date, status date and last update. Unknown values stay blank rather than being inferred. A community route proposal links to its issue and creator; it is not evidence of the true alignment. Later revisions remain visible through the public repository history.</p></section>
      <section id="contribute"><div class="section-label">05 / CONTRIBUTIONS</div><h2>Open submissions, visible provenance.</h2><p>GitHub Issue Forms are checked for structure and recorded on the public-data branch when valid. These checks do not establish factual truth; community-submitted claims remain open to correction. The website and data live in one open repository; the application code is MIT licensed and geographic assets retain their own terms.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="/projects">Explore the projects <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
